•	Bikes travelling up to 60 km/h have no fine.
•	Cars travelling up to 80 km/h have no fine.
•	Commercial vehicles travelling up to 60 km/h have no fine.
•	Exceeding the speed limit by 1–20 km/h results in a ₹1,000 fine.
•	Exceeding it by 21–40 km/h results in a ₹2,000 fine.
•	Exceeding it by more than 40 km/h results in a ₹5,000 fine.
•	Drivers with more than three previous violations receive an additional 50% fine.
Write a Python program to calculate the traffic fine.

vehicle = input("Enter vehicle type: ").lower()
speed = int(input("Enter current speed: "))
violations = int(input("Enter previous violations: "))
if vehicle == "bike":
    limit = 60
elif vehicle == "car":
    limit = 80
elif vehicle == "commercial":
    limit = 60
else:
    limit = -1
if limit == -1:
    print("Invalid Vehicle Type")
elif speed <= limit:
    print("No Fine")
else:
    excess = speed - limit
    if excess <= 20:
        fine = 1000
    elif excess <= 40:
        fine = 2000
    else:
        fine = 5000
    if violations > 3:
        fine *= 1.50
    print("Speed Limit:", limit)
    print("Exceeded Speed:", excess)
    print("Total Fine: ₹", fine)
